== Cross-wave semantic audit ==

=== Internet / Web / platform layer ===

The merged corpus preserves:

<pre>
Internet ≠ World Wide Web
Internet protocol suite ≠ Internet
DNS ≠ Web
URI ≠ network location necessarily
HTTP ≠ HTML
HTTP ≠ Web
client/server role ≠ permanent machine identity
web browser ≠ search engine
web server ≠ physical machine necessarily
API ≠ protocol necessarily
authentication ≠ authorization
interoperability ≠ identical implementation
federation ≠ decentralization
federation ≠ single sign-on
online platform ≠ Internet
moderation action ≠ proof of falsity
</pre>

[[Interoperability]] becomes a useful bridge into Wave 14 FAIR/data infrastructure without collapsing technical interoperability into semantic correctness.

[[Governance]] remains broader than platform governance and content moderation.

=== Scholarly communication / open research layer ===

The merged corpus preserves:

<pre>
scientific literature ≠ all scientific knowledge
scholarly communication ≠ academic publishing only
peer review ≠ proof of correctness
peer review ≠ replication
citation ≠ endorsement
citation count ≠ quality
academic publishing ≠ peer review
preprint ≠ peer-reviewed article
open access ≠ open science
free-to-read ≠ unrestricted reuse necessarily
open science ≠ compulsory disclosure of all research
dataset ≠ database
research data management ≠ data sharing only
data repository ≠ database
repository deposit ≠ preservation guarantee
FAIR ≠ open
FAIR ≠ data quality
FAIR ≠ one implementation standard
</pre>

Publication state, review state, access state, and FAIR status are therefore provenance/management properties rather than truth labels.

=== Wave 13 → Wave 14 ===

The final waves connect through durable general concepts rather than arbitrary adjacency:

* [[Interoperability]] supports FAIR and repository/data exchange without defining data quality.
* [[Authentication]] and [[Authorization]] provide access-control concepts for restricted research data.
* [[Uniform Resource Identifier]] and Web infrastructure support persistent scholarly identification and discovery without becoming citation itself.
* [[Online platform]] and [[Content moderation]] remain general platform concepts; scholarly communication systems may use platforms without being reducible to them.
* [[Governance]], [[Metadata]], [[Provenance]], [[Database]], [[Version control]], and [[Reproducibility]] remain reusable prior-wave foundations.

